# Line endings are decided here, by the repo, not by whoever happens to be
# cloning it.
#
# The first commit was normalised to LF only because this machine's *system*
# gitconfig sets core.autocrlf=true. That is a per-machine setting: a CI runner,
# a Linux collaborator, or a fresh Git install without it would start committing
# CRLF and every file would show up as entirely rewritten. This file makes the
# behaviour a property of the project instead.
#
# text=auto  -> git decides text vs binary, and stores text as LF
# eol=lf     -> check out as LF everywhere, Windows included
#
# LF in the working tree matters beyond tidiness: the shell scripts and
# Dockerfiles here are read by Linux containers, where a trailing CR is a syntax
# error rather than whitespace.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the files where a stray CR actually breaks something rather than
# just looking untidy.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.bash    text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf

# Generated and enormous: keep it out of diffs and out of language stats.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated=true

# Binaries — never touch these. Recipe images land under uploads/ (gitignored),
# but the frontend's public/ folder can pick up icons and fonts.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.webp    binary
*.avif    binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
*.ttf     binary
*.eot     binary
